
Changes with Worker 0.2.0                                        08 Sep 2026

    *) Change: the status API now exposes processes, requests, responses,
       and latency at the top level and for each application. Global
       statistics cover only currently configured application instances;
       removing or replacing an application removes its previous statistics
       from the totals. Connection statistics were removed.

    *) Feature: application process statistics include stopping processes
       separately from running and idle processes, until the router receives
       a process removal notification.

    *) Change: request statistics expose total, waiting, processing, and
       completed. Total counts application dispatches, excluding requests
       rejected before dispatch. Waiting and processing replace active;
       completed includes errors, cancellations, and WebSocket upgrades.

    *) Feature: response statistics count 1xx through 5xx statuses when
       final headers are prepared for sending, including Worker-generated
       errors for an application. The 1xx class counts 101 upgrades only.
       Later body errors do not add a response.

    *) Feature: processing latency statistics expose p50, p95, and p99 in
       milliseconds over a rolling 60-second window. Samples cover ended
       acknowledged requests and exclude waiting time. Global percentiles
       use merged application histograms. Short durations use the
       high-resolution monotonic clock where available.

    *) Bugfix: Go application processes could remain blocked after reaching
       the configured request limit. Removed ports now close their sockets,
       and the application library handles end-of-file notifications without
       retrying indefinitely.

    *) Feature: Linux runtime packages include a combined Python/PHP bundle,
       a checksum-verifying installer, persistent launcher state, and
       configuration and status commands.


Changes with Worker 0.1.0                                        05 Sep 2026

    *) initial release of Worker, an application server for PHP, Python, Go,
       and Ruby.

    *) Feature: dynamic application configuration through a JSON control
       API, with process scaling, on-demand process management, and
       application restarts.

    *) Feature: HTTP serving over IPv4, IPv6, and Unix sockets. Applications
       without targets require one listen address. Python and PHP
       applications with targets require one listen address per target and
       no application-level listen. Addresses are unique across all
       applications and targets; targets share their application's process
       pool.

    *) Feature: listen address updates preserve application processes and
       in-flight requests. A failed bind leaves the previous configuration
       active.

    *) Feature: request, connection, and application process statistics
       through the status API.

    *) Feature: Linux Python and PHP runtime packages for amd64 and arm64,
       available as tar.gz archives and deb packages with the language
       runtime and shared libraries included.
